               <msContents>
                  <msItem>
                     <title key="work_3366">The Five Scrolls and Hafṭarot</title>
                     <title xml:lang="he" key="work_3366">מגלות והפטרות</title>
                     <title xml:lang="he-Latn-x-lc">Megillot ṿe-Hafṭarot</title>
                     <textLang mainLang="he" otherLangs="jpa">Hebrew; Jewish Palestinian
                        Aramaic</textLang>
                     <note>The Five Scrolls are accompanied by the Aramaic Targum.</note>
                     <note>The Book of Ruth, Song of Songs and the beginning of Ecclesiastes are
                        accompanied by the commentaries of <persName key="person_19684048"
                           >Rashi</persName> and <persName key="person_90633023">ibn Ezra</persName>
                        on the margins.</note>
                  </msItem>
               </msContents>
               <physDesc>
                  <objectDesc form="codex">
                     <supportDesc material="perg">
                        <support>parchment</support>
                        <extent n="233">ff. 233 <dimensions type="leaf" unit="mm">
                              <height>365</height>
                              <width>225</width>
                           </dimensions>
                        </extent>
                        <foliation><p>Ff. 37-38 (one bifolium) have been misbound; f. 37 should
                              precede f. 29, while f. 38 should follow f. 36.</p>
                           <p>F. 69 is blank.</p></foliation>
                     </supportDesc>
                  </objectDesc>
                  <handDesc>
                     <summary/>
                     <handNote>Ashkenazic square script for the biblical text and the Targum;
                        Italian semi-cursive script for the marginal commentaries (probably by a
                        different hand); Ashkenazic semi-cursive script for the scribe's final
                        colophon (<locus>f. 233r</locus>).</handNote>
                     <handNote>The scribe, <persName>Yaʿaḳov b. Natan of
                              <placeName>Évreux</placeName>
                           <foreign xml:lang="he">יעקב בן נתן מיאייברא</foreign></persName>, left
                        colophons on <locus>ff. 99v, 233r</locus>.</handNote>
                     <handNote>The colophon of the vocaliser, <persName>Yehosef b. Shabbetai b.
                              Shlomoh<foreign xml:lang="he">יהוסף בן שבתי בן
                           שלמה</foreign></persName>, is found on <locus>f. 230v</locus>.</handNote>
                  </handDesc>
                  <decoDesc>
                     <p>The initial word <foreign xml:lang="he">ויהי</foreign> at the beginning of
                        the Book of Esther (<locus>f. 1r</locus>) is painted red.</p>
                  </decoDesc>
                  <bindingDesc>
                     <p>Vellum over paper boards, rebacked in smooth orange-brown leather; a red
                        label bearing the current shelfmark at base of spine.</p>
                     <condition>Small repairs, in the same orange-brown leather as the spine, around
                        the corners and along the edges of both the upper and lower
                        boards.</condition>
                  </bindingDesc>
               </physDesc>
               <history>
                  <origin><origPlace>[Italy]</origPlace><origDate calendar="#Gregorian" when="1327"
                        >1327</origDate></origin>
                  <provenance><p>The manuscript was originally made for <persName>Mosheh b. Shmʾuel
                              <foreign xml:lang="he">משה בן שמואל</foreign></persName>.</p>
                     <p>Ownership inscription: 'Liber Guilielmi Laud Archiepi[scopi]
                        Cantuari[iensis] et Cancellarii Universitatis Oxon[iensis] 1639' (<locus>f.
                           1r</locus>)</p></provenance>
                  <acquisition>It was one of 36 Oriental manuscripts from the collection of Sir
                        <persName key="person_7076">Kenelm Digby</persName> (<date
                        calendar="Gregorian">1603-1665</date>) which was entrusted to Archbishop
                        <persName key="person_54940373">William Laud</persName> for presentation to
                     the Bodleian Library in <date calendar="Gregorian">1640</date>. These
                     manuscripts were inscribed and stamped as if they were Laud's own volumes; they
                     were not separated from the Laud collection until around <date
                        calendar="Gregorian">1885</date>.</acquisition>
               </history>
